sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In ordinary fishes such as cod and haddock (and partly in whales), the backbone is stiffened by three series of ligament-connected processes, dorsal and two transverse, arranged at nearly equal angles of 120°, which gives the greatest and most uniform strength possible for such a system.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Fish vertebral processes are set at about 120° to one another for uniform stiffness";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "three series of processes at \\approx 120^\\circ to one another";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVI, “On Form and Mechanical Efficiency”; topic “Aquatic animals and stiffness”. Scope: vertebral columns of round-bodied fishes and cetaceans"
}
